    <section id="cs-situation">
        <h3>The situation</h3>
        <p>By 2024, teams across the agency were building chat tools, agents, image workflows, and other AI experiments. Each project had its own login, its own copy of client information, and its own decisions about access. A new idea often started by rebuilding setup work another team had already done.</p>
        <p>The agency had an answer on paper. Mod Engine was a proposal for a shared foundation: a bundle of services and data pools, including data about the work happening across the agency, that products could draw on instead of rebuilding. It was a good idea, and it stayed an idea. Nothing yet needed it badly enough to make it real.</p>
        <p>Mod Heat changed that. A useful trend signal depended on user and organization data: the same story could matter differently to two brands, and the people using the product needed the right context and level of access. We needed a way to connect a person to an organization and carry that context into the product experience.</p>
        <p>The larger concern was client trust. We held brand strategy, research, and unreleased work for companies that sometimes competed with one another. A shared platform had to make the boundaries clear to the people building products and to the people using them.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-role">
        <h3>What I owned</h3>
        <p>I led product and technical direction for Nexus with the six-person product and engineering team I managed. I set the product boundary, worked through the access model with the team, and decided which capabilities should become part of the platform. I also worked across UX, data science, transformation, and agency leadership to decide what we built first and how it reached people.</p>
        <p>Nexus began as a response to a product need, so I let live products decide what belonged in the shared layer. Mod Heat gave us the first concrete use case. Each new product made the shared layer more valuable and showed us where it needed to grow. I kept the immediate user problem in view while shaping a platform that could support the portfolio three to six months out.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-foundation">
        <h3>01 &middot; Foundation: Nexus</h3>
        <p>The first version answered Mod Heat's need for user and organization data. We made identity, permissions, and approved company knowledge part of the platform, so products could pick up those capabilities as they took shape. That gave each team more time to focus on the experience it was creating. It was the first working version of the foundation Mod Engine had proposed.</p>
        <p>Nexus tied access to the organization and the user, then carried those rules into retrieval and agent use. This was also the point where Nexus began to host agents. Brand Agents were a natural next step: persistent assistants grounded in approved material and shaped around a specific company or brand, launched publicly in April 2025. Mod Heat could use that context when it interpreted a trend, and the same pattern could support other products and client teams.</p>
        <p>The permission layer later became part of Launchpad's foundation. Prototypes and internal apps could sit behind secure sign-in from their first useful version, with access handled by the platform rather than by each app. That gave Launchpad a reliable way to turn an idea into something people could try and evaluate.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-context">
        <h3>02 &middot; Context: Knowledge Layers + Brand Agents</h3>
        <p>Knowledge Layers let teams collect approved material at the company, brand, or project level. Brand Agents used that material as persistent context. Mod Heat was one of the early products that showed why this mattered: a signal became more useful when the product could understand the brand, the audience, and the team's way of working. The library grew with the platform: more than 60 specialized agents at the April 2025 launch, more than 50 client-specific Brand Agents by that June, and more than 700 agents by Orion's public launch in July 2026.</p>
        <figure class="story-illustration nexus-knowledge-layers-visual">
            <%= image_tag "nexus-knowledge-layers-agent-stack-selected-user-choice.png", alt: "Client organization context, an LLM chosen by the user, and brand-specific system instructions forming a Brand Agent" %>
        </figure>
        <p>Over time, Brand Agents became a shared pattern across the agency. What began as support for Mod Heat was now available to anyone through Nexus Chat, with the relevant context carried into each conversation. By late 2025, client-facing teams were building rapid pretotype agents and integrations on Nexus. By April 2026, people could also bring authorized Nexus context into tools such as Claude Code and Cursor through MCP, the Model Context Protocol. Employees could stay in the tools where they already worked and still draw on the platform's shared knowledge. MCP gave agents a new way to reach data people already had access to, under delegated permissions, with a person approving anything consequential.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-adoption">
        <h3>03 &middot; Adoption: Nexus Chat</h3>
        <figure class="nexus-chat-visual">
            <%= image_tag "mod-chat-nexus-chat.gif", alt: "Mod Op Brand Agent chat interface in Nexus Chat" %>
        </figure>
        <p>Most people encountered Nexus through Nexus Chat. It grew out of the agency's first internal chat tool and became the chat harness where employees could use approved knowledge, agents, and tools from one interface. Access came with a rule: every employee completed training on responsible use before they could use the internal AI tools. As the agent library grew, the harness gave those agents a common place to reach the people who could use them.</p>
        <p>One example was a time-tracking agent that interfaced with NetSuite. It handled the repetitive steps between a question and an update and cut the time most people spent on time tracking each week to a fraction of what it had been. That kind of task gave Nexus a place in a routine people already had, and it gave us a clear standard for what useful automation should feel like.</p>
        <p>I treated adoption as an ongoing product problem. Frontier models were improving continuously, so the product had to keep earning a return visit. I planned on a three-to-six-month horizon: make room for the capabilities models and users would be ready for next, then land them in a familiar surface. People felt the progress in work they already understood.</p>
        <p>That approach created a product-led growth loop inside the organization. Someone could start with a chat, bring a real task to an agent, see the experience improve, and return when the next useful capability arrived. By Orion's public launch in July 2026, adoption stood at 95% of employees, and a large share of the agency was active in Nexus Chat in any given month.</p>
        <p>The retention curve mattered to me. The people who had access longest were the most consistent users, which told us Nexus had settled into real work.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-productization">
        <h3>04 &middot; Packaging: Orion</h3>
        <figure class="nexus-orion-visual">
            <%= image_tag "orion-ecosystem.jpg", alt: "Orion intelligent technology ecosystem" %>
        </figure>
        <p>By July 2026, Nexus and much of the portfolio I owned had become the core of Orion, Mod Op's AI operating layer, which the company <a href="https://www.modop.com/press/mod-op-unveils-orion-a-connected-intelligence-platform-redefining-the-creative-agency-model-for-the-ai-era/" target="_blank">launched publicly on July 28, 2026</a>. Orion connected Nexus, Brand Agents, Mod Heat, Audience Lab, GEO, and Launchpad into one portfolio, with Nexus as the shared base.</p>
        <p>That structure helped agency leaders see how the pieces related, and it gave client teams a clearer way to talk about what we could offer. I owned the six-product portfolio and, in the spring of 2026, helped shape the platform story that became Orion. Nexus gave the portfolio one place where identity, context, agents, and adoption could build on each other as the products evolved.</p>
        <p class="metrics"><a href="https://digiday.com/marketing/build-or-buy-inside-indie-agency-mod-ops-ai-infrastructure/" target="_blank">Digiday covered the build-versus-buy decision</a> in August 2026 and cited roughly $3M in avoided licensing costs across Orion.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-changed">
        <figure class="story-illustration">
            <%= image_tag "mod-heat-working-table.jpg", alt: "Hand-drawn team working together around a table of emerging ideas" %>
        </figure>
        <h3>What changed</h3>
        <p>Nexus began with a need inside Mod Heat for user profile and organization data. It grew into a platform that could host Brand Agents, give every member of the agency access to those agents through Nexus Chat, support useful internal tools such as time tracking, and give Launchpad a secure way to release prototypes and apps.</p>
        <p>By the time Nexus became part of Orion in July 2026, new products started with identity, permissions, and approved context already in place. We planned product delivery alongside adoption and commercial use, and the visible products helped make the case for continued investment in the platform. The result was a shared layer that could absorb the pace of frontier AI and give people a stable place to work.</p>
    </section>

